.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_DESTROY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_destroy \- destroy a ranged memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

ccol_r_mempool_destroy(rmp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Frees every resource of the ranged memory pool, all its sub-pools
included. For a pool created from a preallocated buffer the macro
doesn't free the buffer itself.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
rmp  Ranged memory pool to destroy. The macro sets it to NULL
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Calling the macro with a NULL pointer is safe
For a preallocated pool, the buffer's lifetime is the caller's business

The macro evaluates rmp exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so with an expression
that has a side effect, such as handles[i++], it destroys and clears
one element.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
A pointer from the dynamic fallback that was never freed makes the
destroy fail an assertion, so free every allocated entry before you
destroy the pool.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_r_mempool_create (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_destroy (3)
